  {
   "id": "ZkhyVQLeEHQJ",
   "from": "A.B.",
   "author": "5dd388cc13b3dadc",
   "date": "2015-07-08T20:20:17Z",
   "subject": "Re: Transform individual variables",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I want to show the mean value of the left-right classification per Country and to show this on a world map. </div><div><br></div><div>Its the world value Survey Round 6. Its v2 for countrycode and v95 for the left right classification. <br><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\n\n<br></font></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "jDRiTGKHLcQJ",
   "from": "Jean-Baka Domelevo Entfellner",
   "author": "af271c8777c52f76",
   "date": "2015-07-08T21:05:24Z",
   "subject": "Re: [rworldmap group] Re: Transform individual variables",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi there,\r<br>\r<br>mapCountryData expects to have only one value per country. In your\r<br>case you have ten counts per country, that&#39;s not one value. So you\r<br>want to summarize the distribution. But you cannot ask for the &quot;mean&quot;\r<br>of a factor. Here we expect &quot;Left&quot; to be encoded by 1 and &quot;Right&quot; to\r<br>be encoded as 10, so it seems you took care of giving sound names to\r<br>the levels of your factor, and then we can go\r<br>&quot;mean(as.numeric(wvs$v95))&quot;, or simply &quot;mean(wvs$v95)&quot;, if I assume\r<br>that in your original data frame these are numerical values. And then\r<br>that&#39;s what you will give mapCountryData to plot, and the actual\r<br>crosstable is useless.\r<br>\r<br>So from &quot;wvs&quot; or &quot;wvsneu&quot;, you group by v2 and you summarize by the\r<br>mean of v95 before you give that to plot to mapCountryData. The\r<br>dataframe you give to mapCountryData should have exactly one row per\r<br>country.\r<br>\r<br>With dplyr:\r<br>wvs %&gt;% group_by(v2) %&gt;% summarize(avg=mean(v95)) -&gt; result\r<br>and then you will pass result to joinCountryData2Map and to\r<br>mapCountryData, with nameColumnToPlot=&quot;avg&quot;\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>See for instance\r<br><a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/2547402/standard-library-function-in-r-for-finding-the-mode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/2547402/standard-library-function-in-r-for-finding-the-mode&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494834491000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3S6me1XrbGuQv3N-l_Ox1-\">http://stackoverflow.com/questions/2547402/standard-library-function-in-r-for-finding-the-mode</a>\r<br>in case you want to plot the mode (most frequent value) instead of the\r<br>mean.\r<br>\r<br>Good night,\r<br>   JB\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Wed, Jul 8, 2015 at 10:20 PM, A.B. &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">baiera...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; I want to show the mean value of the left-right classification per Country\r<br>&gt; and to show this on a world map.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Its the world value Survey Round 6. Its v2 for countrycode and v95 for the\r<br>&gt; left right classification.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
